  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read the dashboard and the rule back afterward to confirm them.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' uses the index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, and its time field is `timestamp`. The response code is stored as a string field, `response` / `response.keyword`, not as a number.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-28bdd15b Web logs' (id `s4-28bdd15b-web-logs-a4198c3d3964`) with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart counting requests per time interval.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart counting requests for each response code (top 10 codes).\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-28bdd15b 5xx errors' (id `8ce90b43-396f-409f-a237-49aeed8a21a3`) as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. A request matches when its `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\", which covers every 5xx code. Because the field is a string, this is a text comparison; it gives the right result for 3-digit HTTP codes. I created the rule already enabled.\n4. **Edit and check:** The response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. When I read the rule back it was enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
